From bca62d2920385a42e4d59c9221c8d54fc99efbda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JohnnyC96 <54557697+JohnnyC96@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:03:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Runtime: decode-only GS frontend skips drawing data; DMA
 chain buffer recycled

Two game-thread costs found by sampling with call stacks in Outbreak's
busiest scene (J's Bar), with an external GS drawing:

1. The CPU GS frontend decoded every vertex for a backend that does
   nothing (GSNullBackend) and built a draw batch at every vertex kick
   (writeRegisterPacked + vertexKick + buildDrawBatch: 3.2% of the game
   thread). Now useNullBackendForExternalGS also sets decode-only
   (GS::decodeOnly()): a PACKED tag without an A+D slot is skipped whole,
   only A+D slots are decoded in the others, REGLIST data is skipped (it
   carries only vertex/texture registers), and a vertex kick builds no
   batch. A+D writes (SIGNAL/FINISH/LABEL, CSR, the readback's transfer
   registers) are decoded as before; the external GS still gets every
   packet from the arbiter. setRasterBackend clears it.

2. writeIORegister built each DMA chain in a fresh local vector, growing
   it chunk by chunk and freeing it after processing: memmove/memcpy in
   vector insert, heap allocation and NtFreeVirtualMemory together 13.1%
   of the game thread. The chain buffer is now recycled: the largest
   processed chain's buffer is kept as a spare (capacity retained) for
   the next chain.

Result (outbreak-modern hostprof, 45 s of the busy stretch): copies and
allocation 13.1% -> ~5.3%, GS decoding 3.2% -> below 0.7%; the unpaced
J's Bar check (play.script to load90s) 63 s -> 53 s with the same
capture ticks (6449/8253).

Test (ps2x, PS2ExternalGS): a packet of a PACKED vertex tag, a REGLIST
tag and a PACKED A+D FINISH; with a hook the frontend is decode-only,
skips the first two and still sets CSR FINISH, and the hook gets the
whole packet; without a hook the full decode sets the same FINISH.
Fails without: no GS::decodeOnly (compile error). The buffer recycling
changes no behaviour (the existing DMA chain tests cover the data).
